00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:41.017
I actually have my
Google-versary this week.

00:04:41.017 --> 00:04:41.642
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:41.642 --> 00:04:42.090
MARK MANDEL: Mazel tov.

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:43.170
MELODY MECKFESSEL:
I know, I know.

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:44.253
MELANIE WARRICK: Congrats.

00:04:44.253 --> 00:04:46.230
MELODY MECKFESSEL: And
several years ago I

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:48.960
started working on how to
make developers at Google

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:49.980
more productive.

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:54.390
Where were we wasting time,
how do we make them happy?

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:56.550
And it's really been
this awesome journey

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:59.340
of learning about
how Google has made

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:03.150
one of the most productive and
highest velocity engineering

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:05.550
teams in the world,
and how do we

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:08.400
bring that out to other
communities outside of Google.

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:10.560
So teams doing
open-source development,

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:12.660
teams interacting with
Android and Chrome,

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:14.880
and then also
developers and operators

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:16.830
using Google's Cloud Platform.

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:18.390
So it's this really
awesome blend

00:05:18.390 --> 00:05:21.300
of sharing what we've
learned internally,

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:24.870
collaborating with our partners
to be providing the best

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:27.390
services in the ecosystem,
and then contributing

00:05:27.390 --> 00:05:29.640
to open-source products
that are already

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:32.070
out there that developers
love and care about

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:34.620
or open sourcing things that we
feel like the community would

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:35.980
benefit from.

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:40.110
So what really motivates and
inspires me is how does my team

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:42.794
and how does Google help
influence and improve

00:05:42.794 --> 00:05:44.710
how software engineering
happens in the world?

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:47.760
And it really is about the
people, the developers,

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:49.740
all different
backgrounds, operators,

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:52.170
all different backgrounds,
getting the benefit out

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:53.582
of the work that we've done.

00:05:53.582 --> 00:05:56.040
MELANIE WARRICK: What are some
of the key successes for you

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:59.620
for this year that you've seen
in terms of helping developers?

00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:01.830
MELODY MECKFESSEL: So this
was-- you mentioned Next.

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:05.940
This was kind of our
debut year, a little bit.

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:08.790
We launched Cloud
Source Repositories

00:06:08.790 --> 00:06:11.040
at sort of the next level,
bringing code search,

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:15.750
and Cloud Build integration with
some great open-source partner

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:18.420
work, and then have made
some significant improvements

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:20.670
in Stackdriver, which
provides monitoring

00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:22.650
solutions for operators.

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:25.230
We launched Instant
Response and Management.

00:06:25.230 --> 00:06:28.500
So that's used by Google
SREs as well as operators

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:30.150
using Google's Cloud Platform.

00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:32.430
So it really is about
this end to end experience

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:34.680
for what people call
DevOps and the way

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:36.180
that we think about
DevOps at Google

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:38.700
as automation
around the developer

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:40.270
workflow and the culture.

00:06:40.270 --> 00:06:43.770
So the practices and
the ways in which

00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:45.377
the humans are interacting.

00:06:45.377 --> 00:06:46.960
That was a big thing
for us this year.

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:49.440
I'm pretty excited about it.

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:52.530
And when I look at it, it's
open source, great partner

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:56.010
integration, working
with folks like GitLab

00:06:56.010 --> 00:06:59.590
and integrating with GitHub, and
then putting some products out

00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:01.590
to the marketplace, which
has been a lot of fun.

00:07:01.590 --> 00:07:03.673
MARK MANDEL: Just a couple
of products, not a lot.

00:07:03.673 --> 00:07:04.360
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:05.710
You mentioned something
interesting there, too,

00:07:05.710 --> 00:07:07.960
when you were talking about
the incident response used

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:09.760
both by external
people, like people

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:11.650
who develop on Google
Cloud, as well as SREs.

00:07:11.650 --> 00:07:13.180
Is that a pattern
that we're looking

00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:15.790
to keep going with, where stuff
is both internal and external,

00:07:15.790 --> 00:07:17.659
or bringing stuff
internal to external?

00:07:17.659 --> 00:07:18.700
MELODY MECKFESSEL: It is.

00:07:18.700 --> 00:07:21.450
We've sort of optimized
internal to our environment.

00:07:21.450 --> 00:07:23.830
You know, you think
about Borg, and you

00:07:23.830 --> 00:07:26.380
think about us taking
our storage systems out

00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:29.080
into the market with Cloud
Spanner, right-- great launch

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:30.970
this year-- but I
think there's magic

00:07:30.970 --> 00:07:34.720
that can happen when both
the internal Google teams

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:38.410
and external teams are
using the same products

00:07:38.410 --> 00:07:39.700
and learning from each other.

00:07:39.700 --> 00:07:42.970
We're hearing input
from our customer teams

00:07:42.970 --> 00:07:46.120
that we're then bringing back
to help us innovate internally.

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:49.810
And that's just such a
magical bi-directional sort

00:07:49.810 --> 00:07:52.600
of learning that can happen
between these communities.

00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:55.540
And my goal is to have that
happen as much as we possibly

00:07:55.540 --> 00:07:57.190
can, where we
break down walls so

00:07:57.190 --> 00:07:59.604
that developers and operators
can learn from each other.

00:07:59.604 --> 00:08:00.520
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:03.075
Where do you see this going in
terms of trends, what you've

00:08:03.075 --> 00:08:05.200
seen so far, what do you
think that's going to look

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:07.150
like the next year and beyond?

00:08:07.150 --> 00:08:08.650
MELODY MECKFESSEL:
So probably heard

00:08:08.650 --> 00:08:10.710
about this thing, Kubernetes.

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:11.545
MARK MANDEL: No.

00:08:11.545 --> 00:08:13.170
I don't know anything
about Kubernetes.

00:08:13.170 --> 00:08:15.045
MELANIE WARRICK: Mark
never talks about that.

00:08:15.045 --> 00:08:16.474
[LAUGHTER]

00:08:16.474 --> 00:08:17.890
MELODY MECKFESSEL:
Well, you know,

00:08:17.890 --> 00:08:20.980
amazing engagement
with the community,

00:08:20.980 --> 00:08:25.510
and just looking at the
data of enterprise customers

00:08:25.510 --> 00:08:27.940
experimenting with
Kubernetes and bringing it

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:29.770
into their on-prem environment.

00:08:29.770 --> 00:08:33.280
So our goal in DevOps is to
have an amazing experience

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:35.860
for containers on GCP.

00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:37.510
This is a theme that
we committed to.

00:08:37.510 --> 00:08:40.270
You look at our launches
around vulnerability scanning,

00:08:40.270 --> 00:08:42.159
around the support
in Stackdriver.

00:08:42.159 --> 00:08:45.010
Across the board we
want the best experience

00:08:45.010 --> 00:08:47.560
for using containers
to be on GCP, again,

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:51.220
in a way that's open and builds
on all the great momentum

00:08:51.220 --> 00:08:53.030
that's happening
in the community.

00:08:53.030 --> 00:08:54.940
So that's a trend
that I don't think

00:08:54.940 --> 00:08:58.330
we could have predicted how
quickly teams would be adopting

00:08:58.330 --> 00:09:00.100
use of containers
in their business

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:03.070
and how they write applications
and how they modernize.

00:09:03.070 --> 00:09:05.362
So that's a trend that I see
just up and to the right--

00:09:05.362 --> 00:09:06.278
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:09:06.278 --> 00:09:09.220
MELODY MECKFESSEL: --faster than
we can predict in many ways.

00:09:09.220 --> 00:09:11.980
And when enterprises
are looking to modernize

00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:15.100
their applications, there's
some ridiculous statistic,

00:09:15.100 --> 00:09:17.140
very high, meaning in
the 80%, that they're

00:09:17.140 --> 00:09:20.410
looking to use containers
for modernizing on-prem

00:09:20.410 --> 00:09:22.400
and then as they
come to the cloud.

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:24.640
So that's a trend we're
fully committed to.

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:26.050
It shouldn't be surprising.

00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:28.930
But I think the usage
in terms of adoption

00:09:28.930 --> 00:09:31.150
is happening much faster
than we thought it would.

00:09:31.150 --> 00:09:32.566
So we're paying
attention to that.

00:09:32.566 --> 00:09:35.570
We're looking at adoption, we're
looking at customer feedback.

00:09:35.570 --> 00:09:38.560
The other trend that I
would highlight would be Go.

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:40.600
The Go community is thriving.

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:43.870
The predictions of usage
increase, the feedback

00:09:43.870 --> 00:09:46.390
around respect and
inclusion in the community

00:09:46.390 --> 00:09:50.150
is really inspiring to
me and just awesome.

00:09:50.150 --> 00:09:53.110
And so that, in terms
of Google's contribution

00:09:53.110 --> 00:09:55.480
and participation
to that community,

00:09:55.480 --> 00:09:57.400
really committed to
continuing to make

00:09:57.400 --> 00:10:01.330
Go a great language that
solves a variety of problems

00:10:01.330 --> 00:10:04.270
and works great on GCP.

00:10:04.270 --> 00:10:06.430
The third pattern
that I would highlight

00:10:06.430 --> 00:10:09.730
would be we started this
experiment within Google

00:10:09.730 --> 00:10:11.680
to learn about
developer productivity.

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:13.510
An interesting
part about this is

00:10:13.510 --> 00:10:16.372
that it has both qualitative
and quantitative.

00:10:16.372 --> 00:10:17.830
So we measure
everything at Google,

00:10:17.830 --> 00:10:20.200
right, we're always trying
to learn from the data.

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:22.210
But there's also a
qualitative aspect

00:10:22.210 --> 00:10:26.440
of this, of how happy are the
humans, how satisfied are they?

00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:31.255
And, you know, engineers are
tough to make happy, right?

00:10:31.255 --> 00:10:32.225
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:32.225 --> 00:10:35.796
The general healthy skepticism
and keep raising the bar.

00:10:35.796 --> 00:10:37.170
MELANIE WARRICK:
Spaces and tabs.

00:10:37.170 --> 00:10:37.670
Yeah.

00:10:37.670 --> 00:10:39.185
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:39.185 --> 00:10:40.810
MELODY MECKFESSEL:
So one of the things

00:10:40.810 --> 00:10:42.280
that we're really
excited about is

00:10:42.280 --> 00:10:45.430
bringing some of those
insights into productivity

00:10:45.430 --> 00:10:46.670
out into the market.

00:10:46.670 --> 00:10:48.760
And when I talk to
our partners, we're

00:10:48.760 --> 00:10:52.210
all sort of thinking about
as millions of developers

00:10:52.210 --> 00:10:56.050
come to the cloud, how do we
help have them be productive

00:10:56.050 --> 00:10:59.350
and maybe a little bit happy
and enabled to create and take

00:10:59.350 --> 00:11:04.540
their ideas to reality in a
way that's a good experience?

00:11:04.540 --> 00:11:07.450
So that's a trend that I see
us being very committed to

00:11:07.450 --> 00:11:10.692
in terms of helping developers
and operators on the platform.

00:11:10.692 --> 00:11:13.150
MARK MANDEL: You were talking
a little bit about Kubernetes

00:11:13.150 --> 00:11:14.140
and running anywhere.

00:11:14.140 --> 00:11:15.250
I'd love to hear your
take on where you

00:11:15.250 --> 00:11:16.600
think hybrid cloud is going.

00:11:16.600 --> 00:11:18.100
Is that something
you think is going

00:11:18.100 --> 00:11:19.812
to expand more or contract?

00:11:19.812 --> 00:11:20.770
MELODY MECKFESSEL: Yes.

00:11:20.770 --> 00:11:23.470
So again, we look at
the research data,

00:11:23.470 --> 00:11:25.870
we're pretty committed to
learning from our customers.

00:11:25.870 --> 00:11:28.360
Again, in the
mid-80%, enterprise

00:11:28.360 --> 00:11:30.340
customers are looking
to hybrid cloud.

00:11:30.340 --> 00:11:33.520
They're committing to solutions
that support hybrid cloud.

00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:35.920
Amazing announcement
with GKE On-Prem,

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:38.950
right, great feedback
around that alpha launch

00:11:38.950 --> 00:11:42.160
that's coming in from
a DevOp standpoint,

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:44.740
help the developers and
operators be productive

00:11:44.740 --> 00:11:47.500
whether they're on-prem
or on the cloud.

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:49.042
And we know that
customers are going

00:11:49.042 --> 00:11:51.250
to be making different
decisions around their journey

00:11:51.250 --> 00:11:53.650
to the cloud, so we want
to support them in that

00:11:53.650 --> 00:11:55.420
and empower them in that.

00:11:55.420 --> 00:11:57.400
I think yes and yes,
it will continue

00:11:57.400 --> 00:12:01.750
to be a key priority
for GCP and for making

00:12:01.750 --> 00:12:03.740
developers and operators happy.

00:12:03.740 --> 00:12:04.600
MELANIE WARRICK: So
you'd mentioned before--

00:12:04.600 --> 00:12:07.190
I know we talked already a
little bit about Kubernetes--

00:12:07.190 --> 00:12:09.365
what are some of the
other open-source projects

00:12:09.365 --> 00:12:10.990
that you're particularly
excited about,

00:12:10.990 --> 00:12:14.530
that you may have a bias about,
that are specifically ones

00:12:14.530 --> 00:12:17.320
that you enjoy and inspired by?

00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:20.710
MELODY MECKFESSEL: So I
will highlight a pattern

00:12:20.710 --> 00:12:22.260
that I'm seeing, which is--

00:12:22.260 --> 00:12:22.630
MELANIE WARRICK: I like that.

00:12:22.630 --> 00:12:24.070
MELODY MECKFESSEL:
--not anything specific,

00:12:24.070 --> 00:12:25.694
but I think if you
look at the pattern,

00:12:25.694 --> 00:12:29.200
you can see a bunch of really
cool innovation happening.

00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:31.730
So the CI/CD,
Continuous Integration

00:12:31.730 --> 00:12:35.500
and Continuous Delivery
space, is really fragmented.

00:12:35.500 --> 00:12:38.320
And so what we're
seeing is either

00:12:38.320 --> 00:12:42.006
around Kubernetes or around
aspects of what developers are

00:12:42.006 --> 00:12:43.630
trying to solve these
problems, there's

00:12:43.630 --> 00:12:45.400
these open source-tools
that are coming up

00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:47.620
that are very specific
to particular things

00:12:47.620 --> 00:12:49.300
that developers need to do.

00:12:49.300 --> 00:12:52.960
So an example of that would be
Skaffold, which we talked about

00:12:52.960 --> 00:12:54.190
at Tokyo Next.

00:12:54.190 --> 00:12:55.750
It's focused on
how do we make it

00:12:55.750 --> 00:12:59.530
really easy to run Kubernetes
on your local environment.

00:12:59.530 --> 00:13:01.650
And we know that developers
want to be portable.

00:13:01.650 --> 00:13:03.440
They want to develop anywhere.

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:06.430
So you can see these
bits where, hey, there's

00:13:06.430 --> 00:13:08.590
this pain in my workflow.

00:13:08.590 --> 00:13:11.500
I'm going to develop something
that is open source first,

00:13:11.500 --> 00:13:13.840
that can get contributions
from the community.

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:17.140
Same thing with Minikube,
which was something

00:13:17.140 --> 00:13:19.300
we released a while
ago, but I think

00:13:19.300 --> 00:13:22.690
that part of the
ecosystem is incredibly

00:13:22.690 --> 00:13:26.690
creative and inspiring in that
the community is looking for--

00:13:26.690 --> 00:13:28.900
like, solve specific
problems that's

00:13:28.900 --> 00:13:32.210
grounded in pain that
developers are feeling today.

00:13:32.210 --> 00:13:35.830
And so I'm sure there's big
massive open-source projects

00:13:35.830 --> 00:13:38.350
that we don't even know
about that are on their way,

00:13:38.350 --> 00:13:41.560
but I think I'm sort of inspired
by some of the smaller things

00:13:41.560 --> 00:13:44.620
that I'm seeing that are gaining
traction that are helping

00:13:44.620 --> 00:13:46.112
developers get their job done.

00:13:46.112 --> 00:13:47.320
MELANIE WARRICK: I like that.

00:13:47.320 --> 00:13:47.950
MARK MANDEL: That's very cool.

00:13:47.950 --> 00:13:48.490
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:13:48.490 --> 00:13:50.614
Another question, actually,
that's come to my mind.

00:13:50.614 --> 00:13:52.108
Where did you start out?

00:13:52.108 --> 00:13:54.056
[LAUGHTER]

00:13:54.056 --> 00:13:55.930
MELODY MECKFESSEL: Well,
I guess I should say

00:13:55.930 --> 00:13:58.360
I grew up in rural California.

00:13:58.360 --> 00:14:00.790
So there were
literally eight kids

00:14:00.790 --> 00:14:02.620
in my elementary school class.

00:14:02.620 --> 00:14:06.700
Math and science and computer
science were not top of mind.

00:14:06.700 --> 00:14:09.670
But I feel super grateful
that I had some teachers that

00:14:09.670 --> 00:14:12.310
saw that I was really
interested in math and science

00:14:12.310 --> 00:14:14.830
and through up into
college, really

00:14:14.830 --> 00:14:18.970
supported me in seeking out
how to learn about this area.

00:14:18.970 --> 00:14:22.840
Got to college, wanted to learn
more about computer science,

00:14:22.840 --> 00:14:25.510
at the college I went to
wasn't a great community,

00:14:25.510 --> 00:14:26.845
so I studied some other things.

00:14:26.845 --> 00:14:30.110
I studied about
biology and history.

00:14:30.110 --> 00:14:32.680
And then was
dabbling on the side

00:14:32.680 --> 00:14:34.510
and taking classes
and learning more.

00:14:34.510 --> 00:14:37.780
And started my
career at a startup

00:14:37.780 --> 00:14:40.300
where I was having to do
pretty much everything,

00:14:40.300 --> 00:14:42.310
learn the front end,
the back end, you know,

00:14:42.310 --> 00:14:43.570
full stack developer.

00:14:43.570 --> 00:14:46.030
[LAUGHTER]

00:14:46.030 --> 00:14:47.950
MARK MANDEL: Before,
that was just a job.

00:14:47.950 --> 00:14:48.867
That was what you did.

00:14:48.867 --> 00:14:50.491
MELODY MECKFESSEL:
That was what I did.

00:14:50.491 --> 00:14:53.260
And then I started working
building enterprise software

00:14:53.260 --> 00:14:57.550
for banks and energy
companies and saw

00:14:57.550 --> 00:15:01.570
what happened with the bubble
and bust in Silicon Valley,

00:15:01.570 --> 00:15:05.320
and multiple iterations of that.

00:15:05.320 --> 00:15:07.900
So it's definitely
been quite a journey

00:15:07.900 --> 00:15:10.600
of seeing how the
industry has evolved,

00:15:10.600 --> 00:15:13.510
seeing the patterns of
sort of ebb and flow

00:15:13.510 --> 00:15:17.170
of grounding in the business and
grounding in what users need,

00:15:17.170 --> 00:15:20.080
and also seeing the
tests that have happen.

00:15:20.080 --> 00:15:22.000
There's a big cultural
test, I think,

00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:25.570
going on right now in the valley
around inclusion and diversity.

00:15:25.570 --> 00:15:27.610
I'm really committed
to, in the work

00:15:27.610 --> 00:15:29.290
that we're doing
in DevOps, creating

00:15:29.290 --> 00:15:32.110
the most inclusive and
respectful experience

00:15:32.110 --> 00:15:33.400
for developers and operators.

00:15:33.400 --> 00:15:37.252
So if you think about great
interactions in code review.

00:15:37.252 --> 00:15:39.210
MELANIE WARRICK: And I'll
deviate for a second.

00:15:39.210 --> 00:15:41.590
Are there any specific
things that you recommend

00:15:41.590 --> 00:15:43.690
for those who want to be
more inclusive in regards

00:15:43.690 --> 00:15:46.660
to code reviews and other
types of just bringing people

00:15:46.660 --> 00:15:48.577
into these type of things,
bringing into tech?

00:15:48.577 --> 00:15:51.076
MELODY MECKFESSEL: All-- again,
if we come back to the data,

00:15:51.076 --> 00:15:53.890
all the data shows Google
and many other tech companies

00:15:53.890 --> 00:15:57.100
are building products for
a diverse set of users.

00:15:57.100 --> 00:15:59.350
However, when you look
at the humans that

00:15:59.350 --> 00:16:01.900
are actually building
those products,

00:16:01.900 --> 00:16:04.090
it's not a diverse team.

00:16:04.090 --> 00:16:05.860
So we miss things.

00:16:05.860 --> 00:16:09.820
We miss-- and you can look in
examples of just missing it.

00:16:09.820 --> 00:16:11.470
And we need to do a better job.

00:16:11.470 --> 00:16:13.660
We need to be explicit
and thoughtful

00:16:13.660 --> 00:16:17.420
about the communities and
the users that we're serving.

00:16:17.420 --> 00:16:20.470
And I strongly believe that
diversity of perspectives

00:16:20.470 --> 00:16:23.500
will lead to better products
for the diverse set of users

00:16:23.500 --> 00:16:25.450
that we're trying to help.

00:16:25.450 --> 00:16:29.050
And I think-- so what people
can bring, bring openness.

00:16:29.050 --> 00:16:30.850
Bring curiosity.

00:16:30.850 --> 00:16:32.410
Bring questions.

00:16:32.410 --> 00:16:35.230
Be respectful and open to
different perspectives.

00:16:35.230 --> 00:16:37.995
Listen, we all have a part
to play in every interaction.

00:16:37.995 --> 00:16:39.370
MELANIE WARRICK:
I mean, here you

00:16:39.370 --> 00:16:42.202
have this wonderful robust
extensive experience

00:16:42.202 --> 00:16:43.660
in the space in
general that you're

00:16:43.660 --> 00:16:46.794
able to bring to how
you view, how you lead.

00:16:46.794 --> 00:16:48.460
What are some of the
key skills that you

00:16:48.460 --> 00:16:49.630
feel like you've
been able to gain

00:16:49.630 --> 00:16:51.338
over the last several
years in particular

00:16:51.338 --> 00:16:53.890
that's helped you really
lead and transition more

00:16:53.890 --> 00:16:56.836
as a leader and
not as much as a--

00:16:56.836 --> 00:16:58.210
like, you're
building, but you're

00:16:58.210 --> 00:16:59.911
leading others to build now.

00:16:59.911 --> 00:17:00.910
MELODY MECKFESSEL: Yeah.

00:17:00.910 --> 00:17:03.880
I have had some awesome
allies and mentors

00:17:03.880 --> 00:17:05.619
that I've gone to
for help, that really

00:17:05.619 --> 00:17:08.911
helped me check my own
stories that I would tell.

00:17:08.911 --> 00:17:10.619
I know I've mentioned
this to you before.

00:17:10.619 --> 00:17:11.828
I'm a big fan of Brené Brown.

00:17:11.828 --> 00:17:13.118
MELANIE WARRICK: Yes, you have.

00:17:13.118 --> 00:17:16.040
MELODY MECKFESSEL: And you know,
I'm going to butcher the quote,

00:17:16.040 --> 00:17:17.609
but she has something
like, sometimes

00:17:17.609 --> 00:17:20.579
the most courageous thing
we can do is just show up.

00:17:20.579 --> 00:17:22.672
And I think showing
up as who you are

00:17:22.672 --> 00:17:24.630
is something that I have
learned over the years

00:17:24.630 --> 00:17:28.560
in tech, being the only woman
in the room many, many times,

00:17:28.560 --> 00:17:32.190
to show up in how I want
to model leadership.

00:17:32.190 --> 00:17:35.400
And that comes back
to caring and kindness

00:17:35.400 --> 00:17:37.890
for the people around
me and inspiring

00:17:37.890 --> 00:17:40.770
toward these big,
audacious goals

00:17:40.770 --> 00:17:42.940
that we have in the world.

00:17:42.940 --> 00:17:48.240
I really believe that we work
really hard on these goals.

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:52.800
We spend a lot of time
together, and the experience

00:17:52.800 --> 00:17:55.500
of people working together,
feeling cared for,

00:17:55.500 --> 00:17:59.780
feeling respected, feeling like
their perspective is valued,

00:17:59.780 --> 00:18:02.070
is such an important
part of doing good work.

00:18:02.070 --> 00:18:04.424
And when people don't
feel like that it's

00:18:04.424 --> 00:18:06.090
going to be more
difficult to accomplish

00:18:06.090 --> 00:18:08.170
those big, audacious goals.

00:18:08.170 --> 00:18:10.260
So for me the
transition in leadership

00:18:10.260 --> 00:18:13.230
was embracing
showing up as myself

00:18:13.230 --> 00:18:16.230
and coming from a place
of caring and kindness

00:18:16.230 --> 00:18:19.410
for the people around me and
really showing that to people.

00:18:19.410 --> 00:18:21.780
Along with the technical
debates and the details

00:18:21.780 --> 00:18:23.585
and all that great stuff.

00:18:23.585 --> 00:18:24.555
We can have both.

00:18:24.555 --> 00:18:26.680
MARK MANDEL: And to bring
that full circle as well,

00:18:26.680 --> 00:18:29.322
I'm guessing that feeds back
into as you look to developers

00:18:29.322 --> 00:18:30.780
or people who use
our products, how

00:18:30.780 --> 00:18:32.040
to make them happier as well.

00:18:32.040 --> 00:18:33.040
MELODY MECKFESSEL: Yeah.

00:18:33.040 --> 00:18:35.760
So user experience
research, right?

00:18:35.760 --> 00:18:38.520
Listening to them, listening
to their perspectives.

00:18:38.520 --> 00:18:41.220
Hearing what's working for them,
what's not working for them.

00:18:41.220 --> 00:18:44.410
Coming from a place of
empathy, of how to help them.

00:18:44.410 --> 00:18:47.910
I really believe in the more
we see the pain in the products

00:18:47.910 --> 00:18:50.070
that we're developing,
the more focus we're

00:18:50.070 --> 00:18:53.550
going to have to solving those
problems, which, to come back

00:18:53.550 --> 00:18:56.910
to your point about instant
response and management,

00:18:56.910 --> 00:18:59.910
we need to make sure that
we're addressing the pain

00:18:59.910 --> 00:19:02.077
points for both our
internal communities

00:19:02.077 --> 00:19:03.660
and our external
communities, and that

00:19:03.660 --> 00:19:07.440
keeps us super focused on
making that the best experience

00:19:07.440 --> 00:19:08.430
that we can.

00:19:08.430 --> 00:19:09.805
MELANIE WARRICK:
What do you like

00:19:09.805 --> 00:19:11.960
to hack on when you
have your own downtime?

00:19:11.960 --> 00:19:12.780
MELODY MECKFESSEL: I don't
know that I'm getting

00:19:12.780 --> 00:19:14.030
a lot of downtime these days.

00:19:14.030 --> 00:19:14.846
MELANIE WARRICK:
Probably not lately.

00:19:14.846 --> 00:19:15.600
[LAUGHTER]

00:19:15.600 --> 00:19:17.350
MELODY MECKFESSEL: But
what do I do when I

00:19:17.350 --> 00:19:19.020
have a block of deep work time?

00:19:19.020 --> 00:19:19.936
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:19:19.936 --> 00:19:23.280
MELODY MECKFESSEL: It is
about writing down and then

00:19:23.280 --> 00:19:25.362
getting at the whiteboard
around the things

00:19:25.362 --> 00:19:27.570
that we need to be thinking
about a year or two years

00:19:27.570 --> 00:19:28.230
from now.

00:19:28.230 --> 00:19:32.010
So really pushing ourselves
on the bleeding edge of what

00:19:32.010 --> 00:19:33.570
the experience could be.

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:37.110
I think there's just a lot
of room for improvement

00:19:37.110 --> 00:19:40.410
in how developers
write software,

00:19:40.410 --> 00:19:44.010
and how much toil there is,
and there's some things a year

00:19:44.010 --> 00:19:47.100
or two out around how to
have the experiences be

00:19:47.100 --> 00:19:50.310
more natural, more
insightful, more intuitive,

00:19:50.310 --> 00:19:53.610
include more automation,
that I want us to work on so.

00:19:53.610 --> 00:19:56.490
When I have downtime
I write and whiteboard

00:19:56.490 --> 00:19:58.200
and start to put
the ideas out there

00:19:58.200 --> 00:19:59.910
so that we can iterate on them.

00:19:59.910 --> 00:20:00.630
MELANIE WARRICK: And strategize.

00:20:00.630 --> 00:20:01.300
MELODY MECKFESSEL: Yeah.

00:20:01.300 --> 00:20:03.000
MELANIE WARRICK: I know you
were talking about Brené Brown

00:20:03.000 --> 00:20:05.880
in particular as a resource,
as somebody who's inspired,

00:20:05.880 --> 00:20:08.880
but when you're thinking about
what's next and where things

00:20:08.880 --> 00:20:11.790
are going, what are some
resources and people and tools

00:20:11.790 --> 00:20:14.790
that you turn to help
you get some ideas?

00:20:14.790 --> 00:20:18.720
MELODY MECKFESSEL: I turn to
research and internal data

00:20:18.720 --> 00:20:19.896
and external data.

00:20:19.896 --> 00:20:21.270
So there's an
organization called

00:20:21.270 --> 00:20:25.080
DORA, which is the DevOps
research group, led

00:20:25.080 --> 00:20:29.310
by an awesome team, Nicole
and Jez and Gene and Soo,

00:20:29.310 --> 00:20:31.950
they have been studying
DevOps practices

00:20:31.950 --> 00:20:35.520
and what makes DevOps teams
perform well for a long time.

00:20:35.520 --> 00:20:38.280
They just released a
report, so that's something

00:20:38.280 --> 00:20:41.535
that Google helped sponsor
and are a big believer in,

00:20:41.535 --> 00:20:43.620
in terms of understanding
the practices.

00:20:43.620 --> 00:20:44.645
So I look to that.

00:20:44.645 --> 00:20:46.770
One of the themes that came
up in the latest report

00:20:46.770 --> 00:20:48.930
was security, and this
idea of shifting left.

00:20:48.930 --> 00:20:52.080
Like how can you move
security closer to code,

00:20:52.080 --> 00:20:55.660
than you're finding a security
issue out in production?

00:20:55.660 --> 00:20:59.430
So we're really listening to
that external research as a way

00:20:59.430 --> 00:21:02.790
to sort of think about how
we embed security practices

00:21:02.790 --> 00:21:06.480
and policies and automation
closer to the code,

00:21:06.480 --> 00:21:07.950
much farther away
from the end user

00:21:07.950 --> 00:21:09.820
impact, just as an example.

00:21:09.820 --> 00:21:11.200
So I look to research.

00:21:11.200 --> 00:21:12.450
I look to research internally.

00:21:12.450 --> 00:21:15.240
We have a great research
organization team here

00:21:15.240 --> 00:21:18.180
that meets with customers, that
works with external customer

00:21:18.180 --> 00:21:20.520
survey groups, and we
do our own surveys.

00:21:20.520 --> 00:21:22.560
So I'm really looking
to hear from people

00:21:22.560 --> 00:21:26.060
and understand that data as I'm
processing what's next for us.

00:21:26.060 --> 00:21:28.310
MARK MANDEL: Given that
you're doing all that research

00:21:28.310 --> 00:21:30.720
and you mentioned security, what
are the challenges that you're

00:21:30.720 --> 00:21:32.850
actually seeing people are
having out in the wild?

00:21:32.850 --> 00:21:35.230
MELODY MECKFESSEL: Well, one
is that the toil is so high.

00:21:35.230 --> 00:21:35.850
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:21:35.850 --> 00:21:38.224
MELODY MECKFESSEL: So it's
like how do I know what to use

00:21:38.224 --> 00:21:40.710
and how do I create the glue
to keep it all together?

00:21:40.710 --> 00:21:42.210
Lot of pain around that.

00:21:42.210 --> 00:21:44.470
Security is really top of mind.

00:21:44.470 --> 00:21:48.760
So if you think about the
patterns of open source usage,

00:21:48.760 --> 00:21:51.090
again, up and to the
right, enterprise companies

00:21:51.090 --> 00:21:53.700
are adopting open source,
everyone's using it,

00:21:53.700 --> 00:21:55.870
the rate of contributions
is increasing.

00:21:55.870 --> 00:21:59.400
Do we know what our
dependencies are to open source?

00:21:59.400 --> 00:22:01.470
We don't have a lot of
transparency into that.

00:22:01.470 --> 00:22:01.890
MELANIE WARRICK: We don't.

00:22:01.890 --> 00:22:03.640
MELODY MECKFESSEL: So
there's risk, right?

00:22:03.640 --> 00:22:06.850
And that's a problem Google will
help solve in the community.

00:22:06.850 --> 00:22:08.830
So transparency is a big one.

00:22:08.830 --> 00:22:11.160
We don't know what we're
dependent on in the stack.

00:22:11.160 --> 00:22:15.120
Security, transparency,
I think moving quickly.

00:22:15.120 --> 00:22:17.760
So I will say, coming
back to productivity,

00:22:17.760 --> 00:22:20.400
when I talk to customers,
the productivity in some

00:22:20.400 --> 00:22:23.220
of their organizations is low,
and when you do some debugging,

00:22:23.220 --> 00:22:26.430
it could be because the
teams don't trust each other,

00:22:26.430 --> 00:22:28.890
like there's resistance
to releasing software.

00:22:28.890 --> 00:22:31.830
So they have one
release a quarter,

00:22:31.830 --> 00:22:33.900
versus 10 releases a day.

00:22:33.900 --> 00:22:38.160
So you sort of dig into how do
we help teams learn and build

00:22:38.160 --> 00:22:39.210
trust?

00:22:39.210 --> 00:22:43.350
But yeah, I would say security,
transparency, productivity,

00:22:43.350 --> 00:22:46.170
continuing our
focus on automation.

00:22:46.170 --> 00:22:47.800
People are struggling with that.

00:22:47.800 --> 00:22:49.770
And then I think Google
can help play a role

00:22:49.770 --> 00:22:51.900
and embrace standards
that are starting

00:22:51.900 --> 00:22:55.507
to evolve in the different
spaces that we're working.

00:22:55.507 --> 00:22:57.090
MELANIE WARRICK:
Particularly the fact

00:22:57.090 --> 00:22:58.923
that you'd mentioned
about people struggling

00:22:58.923 --> 00:23:01.170
with the automation piece,
it's interesting to watch,

00:23:01.170 --> 00:23:03.402
because I know I've seen
us moving in these fits

00:23:03.402 --> 00:23:04.860
and starts in some
ways of like, we

00:23:04.860 --> 00:23:06.630
want to automate as
much as possible,

00:23:06.630 --> 00:23:09.000
but yet we also recognize
there's only so much that can

00:23:09.000 --> 00:23:10.650
at certain times be automated.

00:23:10.650 --> 00:23:12.780
And it's continually evolving.

00:23:12.780 --> 00:23:15.270
But it's fascinating, and
it's fun to watch, too,

00:23:15.270 --> 00:23:16.196
in a lot of ways.

00:23:16.196 --> 00:23:17.820
MELODY MECKFESSEL:
Another point around

00:23:17.820 --> 00:23:20.530
that is that more so
than any other time,

00:23:20.530 --> 00:23:23.880
60% plus of developers
feel like they have

00:23:23.880 --> 00:23:25.200
a choice in their tool set.

00:23:25.200 --> 00:23:27.750
So the fastest way to
make a developer unhappy

00:23:27.750 --> 00:23:28.740
is to waste their time.

00:23:28.740 --> 00:23:31.198
They're going to pick the thing
that makes them productive,

00:23:31.198 --> 00:23:33.030
like they're empowered.

00:23:33.030 --> 00:23:35.370
So if you look at
automation, they

00:23:35.370 --> 00:23:38.174
want the thing that's going
to help them remove that toil.

00:23:38.174 --> 00:23:39.090
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:23:39.090 --> 00:23:40.950
I mean, especially when you're
doing a thing with machine

00:23:40.950 --> 00:23:43.408
learning and trying to model,
the last thing you want to do

00:23:43.408 --> 00:23:47.010
is spend most of your time just
trying to set up your system.

00:23:47.010 --> 00:23:49.572
You want to just get to
[INAUDIBLE] if anything,

00:23:49.572 --> 00:23:51.780
and then also getting the
data and cleaning the data.

00:23:51.780 --> 00:23:54.279
That's where a large part of
your time will get sucked into,

00:23:54.279 --> 00:23:56.340
and the more you can
actually automate that,

00:23:56.340 --> 00:23:58.350
the faster you can
get to a solution.

00:23:58.350 --> 00:24:00.900
Off of that note, I might as
well go for this question,

00:24:00.900 --> 00:24:03.780
since we are the "Google
Cloud Platform Podcast,"

00:24:03.780 --> 00:24:05.970
are there any cloud
products that particularly

00:24:05.970 --> 00:24:06.769
are your favorites?

00:24:06.769 --> 00:24:08.310
MELODY MECKFESSEL:
Do-- I don't know.

00:24:08.310 --> 00:24:10.060
MARK MANDEL: Do you
have a favorite child?

00:24:10.060 --> 00:24:11.007
[LAUGHTER]

00:24:11.007 --> 00:24:12.340
MELODY MECKFESSEL: No, I think--

00:24:12.340 --> 00:24:13.800
MELANIE WARRICK: Since you
have an emphasis on DevOps,

00:24:13.800 --> 00:24:14.880
maybe there is a favorite there.

00:24:14.880 --> 00:24:16.470
MELODY MECKFESSEL:
I would say I'm

00:24:16.470 --> 00:24:18.600
pretty excited about
the contributions

00:24:18.600 --> 00:24:21.630
that we're making in
the Cloud Build space.

00:24:21.630 --> 00:24:24.480
It really is a combination
of first-party solutions

00:24:24.480 --> 00:24:26.860
that automate and work
well on the platform,

00:24:26.860 --> 00:24:28.870
it's great integration
with our partners,

00:24:28.870 --> 00:24:31.120
there's open-source
components of it,

00:24:31.120 --> 00:24:33.960
you know, Bazel, we open
sourced Google's build system,

00:24:33.960 --> 00:24:35.620
has a part to play in that.

00:24:35.620 --> 00:24:38.670
And I think on the op side,
I'm super inspired by the work

00:24:38.670 --> 00:24:40.950
that the team has done
within Stackdriver

00:24:40.950 --> 00:24:43.710
to bring SRE tools
and practices out

00:24:43.710 --> 00:24:46.170
into the external community.

00:24:46.170 --> 00:24:49.260
I spoke at our Cloud
SRE Summit last week

00:24:49.260 --> 00:24:52.380
and there's just
so much excitement

00:24:52.380 --> 00:24:56.979
about bringing the best
practices, the insights,

00:24:56.979 --> 00:24:59.520
helping people get the insights
so they don't have to go down

00:24:59.520 --> 00:25:02.444
the rabbit hole of debugging
things or struggling,

00:25:02.444 --> 00:25:04.110
which really gets to
the better business

00:25:04.110 --> 00:25:06.390
outcome for those teams, right?

00:25:06.390 --> 00:25:08.612
Minimize the user impact,
that's the end game.

00:25:08.612 --> 00:25:09.570
MELANIE WARRICK: Right.

00:25:09.570 --> 00:25:11.260
MELODY MECKFESSEL:
So I'm super excited.

00:25:11.260 --> 00:25:13.120
I mean, we have a
ton more work to do.

00:25:13.120 --> 00:25:16.690
We're really excited about
the features we're developing.

00:25:16.690 --> 00:25:19.890
And I think 2019 is going
to be an exciting year.

00:25:19.890 --> 00:25:22.181
MELANIE WARRICK: If you
weren't doing what you're doing

00:25:22.181 --> 00:25:24.227
right now, what would you do?

00:25:24.227 --> 00:25:25.560
MELODY MECKFESSEL: I don't know.

00:25:25.560 --> 00:25:27.510
That's a great question.

00:25:27.510 --> 00:25:30.390
I am coaching elementary
school girls' volleyball team,

00:25:30.390 --> 00:25:32.636
and I have to say
it is such a joy.

00:25:32.636 --> 00:25:33.510
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:25:33.510 --> 00:25:34.050
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah?

00:25:34.050 --> 00:25:35.280
MELODY MECKFESSEL: So--

00:25:35.280 --> 00:25:37.321
MELANIE WARRICK: Clearly
different shift in mind?

00:25:37.321 --> 00:25:39.720
MELODY MECKFESSEL: Teaching,
some sort of teaching thing,

00:25:39.720 --> 00:25:40.557
working with people.

00:25:40.557 --> 00:25:42.140
MELANIE WARRICK:
Making things easier.

00:25:42.140 --> 00:25:42.540
MELODY MECKFESSEL: Yeah.

00:25:42.540 --> 00:25:44.190
MELANIE WARRICK: We talked
about this to a degree before,

00:25:44.190 --> 00:25:46.110
but what advice would
you give to those

00:25:46.110 --> 00:25:48.887
who are growing their career
in engineering, especially

00:25:48.887 --> 00:25:49.720
in the DevOps space?

00:25:49.720 --> 00:25:51.928
Is there any advice in
particular you'd give to them?

00:25:51.928 --> 00:25:53.400
Because I know
it's different now,

00:25:53.400 --> 00:25:55.230
still, there's
similar challenges

00:25:55.230 --> 00:25:56.355
of getting into the space.

00:25:56.355 --> 00:25:58.230
MELODY MECKFESSEL: So
there's a couple points

00:25:58.230 --> 00:25:59.190
that I would highlight.

00:25:59.190 --> 00:26:01.710
Really try and find your
passion in the space.

00:26:01.710 --> 00:26:04.230
You know, there's this whole
idea of purpose and passion.

00:26:04.230 --> 00:26:07.890
When I talk with engineers that
are new to the space or new

00:26:07.890 --> 00:26:09.990
in the field, find
something that, really, they

00:26:09.990 --> 00:26:11.170
can connect with.

00:26:11.170 --> 00:26:13.620
And I think from my own
lessons learned in my career

00:26:13.620 --> 00:26:17.250
when I wasn't connected to
the work in some way, inspired

00:26:17.250 --> 00:26:20.854
by it, things didn't really go
as well as I would have liked.

00:26:20.854 --> 00:26:21.895
MARK MANDEL: That's fair.

00:26:21.895 --> 00:26:23.440
[LAUGHTER]

00:26:23.440 --> 00:26:26.182
MELANIE WARRICK: I can
very much relate to that.

00:26:26.182 --> 00:26:28.140
MELODY MECKFESSEL: The
second thing I would say

00:26:28.140 --> 00:26:29.700
is find your people.

00:26:29.700 --> 00:26:31.620
Find the people that
are going to support you

00:26:31.620 --> 00:26:34.050
and challenge you within
your team and outside

00:26:34.050 --> 00:26:37.650
of your team, that are
going to be there to help

00:26:37.650 --> 00:26:39.240
when you have a question.

00:26:39.240 --> 00:26:41.670
And then the final
thing, I think

00:26:41.670 --> 00:26:46.060
it's really important for people
to show up around who they are

00:26:46.060 --> 00:26:47.880
and what they have
to contribute.

00:26:47.880 --> 00:26:51.330
You know, I've been in these
amazing tech design sessions

00:26:51.330 --> 00:26:54.450
where we're at a whiteboard and
someone who maybe doesn't speak

00:26:54.450 --> 00:26:56.940
up all the time
contributes something

00:26:56.940 --> 00:26:58.720
and it shifts the conversation.

00:26:58.720 --> 00:27:02.910
So be willing to take those
risks, to speak up, even when

00:27:02.910 --> 00:27:05.310
you're in early stages of
your career, early stages

00:27:05.310 --> 00:27:07.070
of building your
domain expertise.

00:27:07.070 --> 00:27:09.153
I mean, I think one of the
great things about tech

00:27:09.153 --> 00:27:11.270
is, we're always learning.

00:27:11.270 --> 00:27:12.565
It's always changing.

00:27:12.565 --> 00:27:12.930
MELANIE WARRICK: Agreed.

00:27:12.930 --> 00:27:13.950
MELODY MECKFESSEL:
And so we're all--

00:27:13.950 --> 00:27:15.500
if we have that
beginner's mindset,

00:27:15.500 --> 00:27:17.120
we're all kind of
in the same place.

00:27:17.120 --> 00:27:19.370
We all have imposter syndrome.

00:27:19.370 --> 00:27:21.200
MELANIE WARRICK: Yes, yes we do.

00:27:21.200 --> 00:27:22.340
MARK MANDEL: Except
for one of our friends.

00:27:22.340 --> 00:27:23.420
MELANIE WARRICK: Except
for one who doesn't.

00:27:23.420 --> 00:27:24.140
Yes, you're right.

00:27:24.140 --> 00:27:24.640
[LAUGHTER]

00:27:24.640 --> 00:27:26.210
MARK MANDEL: In
previous episode.

00:27:26.210 --> 00:27:29.390
MELODY MECKFESSEL: But I
mean, I say it all the time.

00:27:29.390 --> 00:27:30.500
Software is a team sport.

00:27:30.500 --> 00:27:31.250
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:27:31.250 --> 00:27:32.666
MELODY MECKFESSEL:
Like, sure, you

00:27:32.666 --> 00:27:33.960
can write software on your own.

00:27:33.960 --> 00:27:37.790
But the fun and the
impact is so much greater

00:27:37.790 --> 00:27:40.040
when you do it together
and you do with a team

00:27:40.040 --> 00:27:42.800
that you have fun and you
can look to for support.

00:27:42.800 --> 00:27:45.452
And so my advice would,
be find that team

00:27:45.452 --> 00:27:46.910
where you're excited
about the work

00:27:46.910 --> 00:27:49.380
that you're doing, that
you can do it together.

00:27:49.380 --> 00:27:51.800
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah,
and off of that also,

00:27:51.800 --> 00:27:53.540
for anybody who's
considering leadership

00:27:53.540 --> 00:27:57.220
in software and tech, any
advice for them specifically?

00:27:57.220 --> 00:27:58.279
[LAUGHTER]

00:27:58.279 --> 00:27:59.570
MELODY MECKFESSEL: Don't do it.

00:27:59.570 --> 00:28:01.153
MARK MANDEL: We ask
the hard questions

00:28:01.153 --> 00:28:03.360
here at the "GCP Podcast."

00:28:03.360 --> 00:28:05.620
MELANIE WARRICK: Only the
ones that make people cry.

00:28:05.620 --> 00:28:08.510
MELODY MECKFESSEL: No--
things are moving really fast.

00:28:08.510 --> 00:28:11.990
So as a leader, we've talked,
just in this short discussion

00:28:11.990 --> 00:28:13.970
today about continuous learning.

00:28:13.970 --> 00:28:14.110
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:28:14.110 --> 00:28:15.943
MELODY MECKFESSEL: You
have to do it, right?

00:28:15.943 --> 00:28:18.050
Staying close to your
user communities.

00:28:18.050 --> 00:28:20.930
Looking for good solutions,
because no company

00:28:20.930 --> 00:28:22.340
is going to do it all.

00:28:22.340 --> 00:28:24.290
So this combination
of first party,

00:28:24.290 --> 00:28:27.500
open source,
partner integration.

00:28:27.500 --> 00:28:29.240
And as someone
coming into the space

00:28:29.240 --> 00:28:32.990
as a leader, sort of being
thoughtful about pulling

00:28:32.990 --> 00:28:35.180
together the resources
to create the best

00:28:35.180 --> 00:28:38.090
offering for the particular
problem that you're solving,

00:28:38.090 --> 00:28:40.280
and doing that in a way
where you're building a team

00:28:40.280 --> 00:28:42.470
that has different perspectives
and different areas

00:28:42.470 --> 00:28:45.110
of expertise to contribute
to that problem.

00:28:45.110 --> 00:28:47.960
So I think being really
open is an important part

00:28:47.960 --> 00:28:52.190
of leadership, being curious,
and then investing in people

00:28:52.190 --> 00:28:55.130
to build a team, because
your job as a leader

00:28:55.130 --> 00:28:58.070
is really to serve your team
and to provide direction

00:28:58.070 --> 00:28:59.397
and guidance and support.

00:28:59.397 --> 00:29:00.980
And so really embracing
that, I think,

00:29:00.980 --> 00:29:03.080
is key to being a
successful leader.

00:29:03.080 --> 00:29:03.950
MARK MANDEL: You were
talking a little bit

00:29:03.950 --> 00:29:05.750
about an empathy, as well,
in the general sense.

00:29:05.750 --> 00:29:08.030
Do you feel that also has a deep
impact as well in leadership

00:29:08.030 --> 00:29:08.520
skills?

00:29:08.520 --> 00:29:09.170
MELODY MECKFESSEL: It does.

00:29:09.170 --> 00:29:10.880
Understanding what
motivates people

00:29:10.880 --> 00:29:13.580
and what people care about
and what they're connected to,

00:29:13.580 --> 00:29:16.310
understanding people are
going through all sorts

00:29:16.310 --> 00:29:17.690
of things outside of work.

00:29:17.690 --> 00:29:19.790
And I think there's
a real opportunity

00:29:19.790 --> 00:29:22.490
to come from a place of
understanding and empathy,

00:29:22.490 --> 00:29:26.480
as you said, with what's going
to help people be their best

