00:30:16.130 --> 00:30:18.470
So question of the
week, if I want

00:30:18.470 --> 00:30:21.500
to configure third-party
notifications such as Slack

00:30:21.500 --> 00:30:23.930
or GitHub into my Cloud
Build configuration,

00:30:23.930 --> 00:30:24.697
how can I do that?

00:30:24.697 --> 00:30:26.030
MARK MANDEL: Excellent question.

00:30:26.030 --> 00:30:27.790
So Cloud Build's really nice.

00:30:27.790 --> 00:30:30.410
So Cloud Build is a continuous
integration deployment platform

00:30:30.410 --> 00:30:32.120
that we have at Google Cloud.

00:30:32.120 --> 00:30:33.740
It's quite flexible
in that what it

00:30:33.740 --> 00:30:36.576
does when it starts
things and ends things,

00:30:36.576 --> 00:30:38.450
et cetera, et cetera,
is it pushes everything

00:30:38.450 --> 00:30:39.800
to a Cloud Pub/Sub topic.

00:30:39.800 --> 00:30:42.200
And then you can listen into
this Cloud Pub/Sub topic

00:30:42.200 --> 00:30:43.430
notifications.

00:30:43.430 --> 00:30:46.730
So when it sends data to a
topic called Cloud Builds,

00:30:46.730 --> 00:30:48.525
you can notify on a
variety of things.

00:30:48.525 --> 00:30:50.150
And one of the easiest
ways of doing it

00:30:50.150 --> 00:30:52.310
is actually just
using Cloud Functions.

00:30:52.310 --> 00:30:56.210
So you can hook a Cloud Function
into a Pub/Sub notification

00:30:56.210 --> 00:30:57.360
and then act upon it.

00:30:57.360 --> 00:30:58.901
So there's actually
an example we're

00:30:58.901 --> 00:31:01.400
going to link to in the show
notes of setting up integration

00:31:01.400 --> 00:31:03.810
with Slack, but you can do
anything with it, essentially.

00:31:03.810 --> 00:31:05.180
So if there's an
external web hook,

00:31:05.180 --> 00:31:06.929
so you might want to
send stuff to GitHub,

00:31:06.929 --> 00:31:09.800
maybe you want to send stuff
to something like SendGrid

00:31:09.800 --> 00:31:12.480
or Mailgun or a variety
of other things in there,

00:31:12.480 --> 00:31:14.930
so from email notifications
to all sorts of other stuff,

00:31:14.930 --> 00:31:16.880
or just making
interesting things

00:31:16.880 --> 00:31:18.754
happen inside your
system, you can do that.

00:31:18.754 --> 00:31:20.420
So, yeah, Cloud
Functions is a great way

00:31:20.420 --> 00:31:22.640
to test this stuff out and
get a nice little easy way

00:31:22.640 --> 00:31:23.450
of building it.

00:31:23.450 --> 00:31:24.710
One other cool thing
I like about this,

00:31:24.710 --> 00:31:26.126
too, is if you
really want to, you

00:31:26.126 --> 00:31:27.542
can do it all in Cloud Shell.

00:31:27.542 --> 00:31:29.750
Because it's JavaScript,
it's not hugely complicated,

00:31:29.750 --> 00:31:31.340
you're usually only
building one function.

00:31:31.340 --> 00:31:32.690
So I actually do that myself.

00:31:32.690 --> 00:31:35.540
I just set up a little source
repository with my functions

00:31:35.540 --> 00:31:37.400
in it and then I just
use the inbuilt editor

00:31:37.400 --> 00:31:38.858
with Cloud Shell,
and it's actually

00:31:38.858 --> 00:31:40.250
pretty nice little workflow.

00:31:40.250 --> 00:31:41.450
MELANIE WARRICK: When you've
